# Turkish → IPA transliteration rules (tr_ipa.rules)
#
# Source-Authors: Zimmer, K. & Orgun, O.
# Source-Year: 1992
# Source-Title: Turkish
# Source-Container: Journal of the International Phonetic Association 22(1-2): 43-45
# Source-Id: https://doi.org/10.1017/S0025100300004588
#
# Additional reference: Berkeley TELL transcription sheet
#
# NFC output (Normalized Form Composed)

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 0.  MACROS  (letters in the *source* alphabet, case-sensitive)
$Vsrc  = [a e ı i o ö u ü A E I İ O Ö U Ü];   # all vowels

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 1.  SOFT-G  (Ğ/ğ   "yumuşak g")
# Per TELL: "vowel length when syllable-final and silence when syllable-initial"
#
# Syllable position is decided by what FOLLOWS. Turkish syllabifies VCV as
# V.CV, so a soft-g between vowels is an onset and TELL gives it silence;
# one before a consonant or a word boundary is a coda and lengthens the
# vowel before it. Zimmer & Orgun agree on the phonetics: lengthening
# "when word-final or followed by a consonant", and "elsewhere when
# intervocalic, it is phonetically zero" (p. 44).

# 1 a. syllable-initial (a vowel follows): silence
ğ } $Vsrc > ;
Ğ } $Vsrc > ;

# 1 b. syllable-final (after a vowel, nothing else matched): lengthening
$Vsrc { ğ } > ː ;
$Vsrc { Ğ } > ː ;

# There is no word-initial case to handle: no Turkish word begins with
# the letter, which is why rule 1 b can take "after a vowel" as its only
# condition.
#
# Corroborated: Csató & Johanson (2021, 'Turkish', The Turkic Languages,
# 2nd ed., Routledge, 195-217, https://doi.org/10.4324/9781003243809-13)
# print ‹dağ› [dɑː] and ‹düğme› [dyːmε] (p. 195), one word per context
# above. Pinned in test_turkish_routledge_words.py.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 2.  DIRECT ORTHOGRAPHY → IPA MAP  (one-to-one segmental rules)

A > a ;  a > a ;
B > b ;  b > b ;
C > d͡ʒ ; c > d͡ʒ ;
Ç > t͡ʃ ; ç > t͡ʃ ;
D > d ;  d > d ;
E > e ;  e > e ;
F > f ;  f > f ;
G > ɡ ;  g > ɡ ;
Ğ > ː ;  ğ > ː ;            # overridden by soft-g rules above
H > h ;  h > h ;
I > ɯ ;  ı > ɯ ;
İ > i ;  i > i ;
J > ʒ ;  j > ʒ ;
K > k ;  k > k ;
L > l ;  l > l ;
M > m ;  m > m ;
N > n ;  n > n ;
O > o ;  o > o ;
Ö > ø ;  ö > ø ;
P > p ;  p > p ;
R > ɾ ;  r > ɾ ;
S > s ;  s > s ;
Ş > ʃ ;  ş > ʃ ;
T > t ;  t > t ;
U > u ;  u > u ;
Ü > y ;  ü > y ;
V > v ;  v > v ;
Y > j ;  y > j ;
Z > z ;  z > z ;

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 3.  THE SECOND LATERAL
#
# Zimmer & Orgun give Turkish two lateral phonemes, "a palatalized
# postalveolar lateral" and "a velarized dental lateral", the velarized
# one of which "does not occur after front vowels" (p. 44). The
# orthography writes both with <l>, so a grapheme-to-phoneme map that
# distinguished them would have to compute the distinction from the
# surrounding vowels rather than read it off the spelling.
#
# These rules do not: <l> is the plain lateral everywhere, one of the
# three declared departures from the Illustration. The lost contrast is
# pinned in the inventory test.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ADDITIONS 2025-12-02: Missing chars found in OSCAR corpus audit
# See docs/ipa-rules-refactor.md
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Circumflex vowels (Arabic/Persian loans, older orthography)
# Indicate length or palatalization of preceding consonant
Â > aː ;  â > aː ;
Î > iː ;  î > iː ;
Û > uː ;  û > uː ;

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

:: NFC ;
